#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a27b27e-8a17-5852-9096-a708286b94aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333785} {:answer/id #uuid "494a9691-afcf-5fee-aee1-319ac2909060" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333786} {:answer/id #uuid "1a7e88cd-d63c-5ccd-b7d4-1f117bcc2ffe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333787} {:answer/id #uuid "d68774e8-d101-544a-a124-cf3218cebb25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333788} {:answer/id #uuid "8ace327a-6c6c-5959-9dc4-a3b54ea1968b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a27b27e-8a17-5852-9096-a708286b94aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333785} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc519def-1ab4-591d-aded-d7d9b64ed698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "750bd076-cd76-5f0d-b037-66703830aec4" :question/math-academy-id "q-129339" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\cancel{(x-y)}}{x\\cancel{(x-y)}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60f588ac-ff24-55a9-a5b2-1e0eb387daa1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333792} {:answer/id #uuid "382754f9-eda8-5cbe-ae49-6ad9086b2517" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333793} {:answer/id #uuid "7e5be2a9-b510-54df-aeb2-e8c45c6b8a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333794} {:answer/id #uuid "1a97865a-2125-5fc7-9a01-47934070f16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333795} {:answer/id #uuid "f31e2527-759d-5be9-8d95-3c62f56c3130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a97865a-2125-5fc7-9a01-47934070f16e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333795} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5adf6ff-a8bb-53c5-ac42-8f3743f775b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddb79f72-2457-5b61-a7fe-eba911b8cffa" :question/math-academy-id "q-129342" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\cancel{x-y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d53554f-6d77-56f9-8950-d8e31a7f4fdb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b22613c0-519c-517b-8cb5-43f8e36cd83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333799} {:answer/id #uuid "7169a415-04ee-5d08-be0c-2033aeca4c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333800} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6904e1-8a84-580f-a552-d5f7e17bde7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333801} {:answer/id #uuid "35f498fc-15fe-5a60-9519-9c786140c0f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333802} {:answer/id #uuid "44dcc4f6-d2cc-54a7-b260-ce6ca40e4fdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6904e1-8a84-580f-a552-d5f7e17bde7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333801} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc263130-fd46-5e0f-b17a-8c778b502b92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0ec61276-c0db-5800-92a2-3727aaf3b851" :question/math-academy-id "q-129348" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}-3}{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-3}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}-3)(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9-9}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+9}+3} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{({0}^{2})({0}^{2})+9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5666035-199d-56aa-a6f4-f65a3f847b42" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits of Multivariable Rational Functions by Factoring"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9afd34f-ccd5-58fe-bc41-91a26f018a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333806} {:answer/id #uuid "d0427c4d-cba6-54c1-b7a1-c1b5106d2c95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333807} {:answer/id #uuid "1406d214-c7e6-59b0-9925-2ad21076e95f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333808} {:answer/id #uuid "f7277171-6f35-5e20-bb12-b32d76f9a6e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333809} {:answer/id #uuid "cbed2f3a-f367-5000-9a60-fc2bdd84a40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbed2f3a-f367-5000-9a60-fc2bdd84a40b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffddc5a6-256c-5054-82f2-b92be4268a10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "834ad829-4477-522b-95e1-81bfa2abc5d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-36109" :question/problem "${lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy}=\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2-2\\cdot {2}^{2}}{2\\cdot {1}^{2}-1\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first factoring the denominator, and then canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy-2{y}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-xy} & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+4xy-xy-2{y}^{2}}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{2x(x+2y)-y(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{(2x-y)(x+2y)}{x(2x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{(x,y)\\to (1,2)}\\frac{x+2y}{x} & =\\frac{1+2\\cdot 2}{1} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186333811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "29610715-333d-5e0c-9328-eec1d45f56ed" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Limits Along Different Paths for Multivariable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "536de9bd-b925-5fde-9a18-7659e6ac81d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333813} {:answer/id #uuid "0e698b23-e674-562b-9725-672ace33dc69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333814} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4df8ba-9dfc-5923-84a3-872dca158f01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333815} {:answer/id #uuid "071f479b-04d2-5caa-b345-9f4513703d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333816} {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cedb535-b6d1-504a-9105-51c5e70ffc2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333817} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d78575b-a3e0-576b-b5ff-36e8ed92f2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "538bab68-7058-5f4f-b133-c515348f8f89" :question/math-academy-id "q-74964" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $2$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $2$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\frac{2\\cdot {0}^{2}}{2\\cdot {0}^{2}-0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x(2x-1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2x}{2x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 0}{2\\cdot 0-1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is also true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{2}-y}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}]}
